          "text": "**Observations**\n\n*Where the first‑layer weights live.* Image row 0 (inputs 0–27), columns 0–2 and columns 26–27 of every row, and the last few inputs of row 27 contain only initialization‑scale noise (|w| ≲ 0.035). Everything else is structured. Strong weights (|w| up to 0.4–0.6) already appear at image rows 1–3 (inputs 32–51, 59–81, 87–110) spanning columns ~3–25, and they persist down to rows 25–27 (inputs ~705–726, 734–754, 767–781). So the \"always‑black\" border is only ~1–2 pixels wide on the sides and essentially nonexistent at the top and bottom: the objects fill the whole 28×28 frame.\n\n*Shape of the structure.* The weights vary smoothly from pixel to neighbouring pixel in long runs of the same sign — wide horizontal bands and solid blobs, not thin stroke‑like templates:\n- Rows 2–3: a horizontal band across columns 5–25 where unit 1 (≈+0.2…+0.36), units 3, 8, 9, 13 are positive and unit 10 is strongly negative (−0.15…−0.40) — a detector for a straight top edge of a wide object.\n- Rows 25–27: a horizontal band across columns 5–25 where unit 7 is positive (+0.3…+0.4), unit 1 (−0.2…−0.39), unit 2 (−0.3…−0.46) and unit 15 (−0.2…−0.4) are negative — a bottom‑edge detector.\n- Right edge, columns ~22–24, rows ~7–13: a vertical blob where units 11, 12, 15 are strongly positive (0.4–0.6) and units 0, 1, 6, 8, 10 are strongly negative.\n- Left edge, columns ~3–5, rows ~15–21: unit 0 strongly negative (−0.35…−0.42), units 2, 3, 15 positive — a lower‑left protrusion detector.\n- Central columns ~9–14 in the lower‑middle rows: unit 0 positive (+0.2…+0.38), with units 9/10 forming opposing central blobs — a \"narrow vertical object in the middle vs. wide object\" contrast.\n\n*Output layer.* W2 is dense with values up to ±0.95; every class draws on many hidden units with mixed signs (e.g., unit 0 → +0.98 for class 8, −0.94 for class 5; unit 7 → +0.79 for class 9, −0.81 for class 0). Output biases are small (|b2| < 0.35). The classifier works by combining coarse region detectors (top band, bottom band, left/right edge blobs, central column), which is what you get for grey‑level silhouettes of whole objects.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nMNIST digits are size‑normalised to a 20×20 box and centred in 28×28, so a network trained on MNIST has near‑zero (init‑noise) weights in rows 0–3 and 24–27 and the outer 3–4 columns. Here rows 1–3 and 25–27 carry some of the largest weights in the whole matrix, so MNIST is ruled out. Handwritten‑character sets like EMNIST or KMNIST also leave a border and their useful weights look like thin stroke fragments, not 15‑pixel‑wide uniform bands. The pattern here — objects that reach the top and bottom edges with straight horizontal boundaries, lateral protrusions at both sides, a left‑bottom protrusion, and a central narrow‑vs‑wide contrast — matches grey‑scale clothing silhouettes scaled to fill the frame: shoulder/neck line of tops and waistband of trousers at the top, hems and soles at the bottom, sleeves at the left/right edges, the leftward‑pointing toe of shoes in the lower left, and narrow items (trousers, dresses) vs. wide ones (coats, bags). The 2‑pixel side border and 0–1‑pixel top/bottom border is exactly the Fashion‑MNIST preprocessing (longest side resized to 28, centred). Ten classes, 28×28 grey, pixel‑filling smooth shapes → Fashion‑MNIST.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion‑MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes)\n\n**Classes:** 0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot\n\n**Confidence:** 75%",
